  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=132",
      "html": "<ul><li>Common name: Fog Talking Catfish</li><li>Temperament: peaceful</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=132",
      "html": "<p>Eastern Ecuador and Peru. Amazon, Upper Amazon. Type locality: Río Bobonaza between Montalvo and Chicherato, Ecuador.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=132",
      "html": "<p>A PVC pipe around about a third longer and wider the fish is ideal. Several fish will happily share a slightly larger pipe. Bogwood or stones can also be used. Care must be taken that the fish does not decide to hide too close to an unguarded heater.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=132",
      "html": "<p>A typical doradid in that it will eat pretty much any food offered.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=132",
      "html": "<p>Peaceful.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=132",
      "html": "<p>Unknown.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=132",
      "html": "<p>Unknown. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=132",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: Rhinodoras comes from the Greek rhinos, meaning nose, and doras, meaning skin (also a word commonly used in forming generic names for doradids); in reference to the unusual snout morphology. Named for the American ichthyologist James Böhlke (1930–1982). Described by Glodek, Whitmire &amp; Orcés V., 1976.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
